{"type": "FeatureCollection", "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Georgia Railroad and Banking Company", "line": "Georgia Railroad and Banking: Main Line", "id": "georgia-railroad-main-line", "color": "#C87137", "mode": "rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 40, "speed_source": "estimated typical steam/early-diesel-era mainline speed for a mixed passenger-freight service; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1838, "end": 1845, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "Georgia Railroad and Banking Company built west from Augusta, reaching Union Point in 1838 and Terminus (renamed Atlanta in 1845) in September 1845, completing the through Augusta-Atlanta route -- one of the earliest trunk railroads in the Deep South.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Georgia_Railroad_and_Banking_Company"]}, {"start": 1845, "end": 1881, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "Operated independently by the Georgia Railroad and Banking Company. Heavily damaged by Sherman's March to the Sea in November 1864 (track, bridges, and rolling stock destroyed along much of the line); rebuilt and back in full operation by the late 1860s.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Georgia_Railroad_and_Banking_Company"]}, {"start": 1881, "end": 1902, "headway_min": 720, "note": "Leased jointly from 1881 to the Central Railroad of Georgia and the Louisville & Nashville Railroad, which operated it as the \"Georgia Railroad\" jointly-controlled property while the Georgia Railroad and Banking Company retained ownership.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Georgia_Railroad_and_Banking_Company"]}, {"start": 1902, "end": 1971, "headway_min": 480, "note": "The Atlantic Coast Line acquired control of the Louisville & Nashville in 1902, bringing the Georgia Railroad lease under the ACL/L&N system. The Atlanta-Augusta run became known for the \"Georgia Cannonball,\" a mixed passenger-and-freight train.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Georgia_Railroad_and_Banking_Company"]}, {"start": 1971, "end": 1983, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "The Georgia Railroad did not join Amtrak in 1971 and kept running its own mixed train, becoming nationally known as one of the last scheduled mixed trains in the US. The Cannonball made its final run on May 6, 1983, ending 138 years of Georgia Railroad passenger service between Atlanta and Augusta.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Georgia_Railroad_and_Banking_Company"]}]}, "geometry": {"type": "LineString", "coordinates": [[-84.3928083, 33.755064], [-84.3889207, 33.7521771], [-84.3842412, 33.7494868], [-84.3813365, 33.7489203], [-84.3780423, 33.7491491], [-84.365055, 33.7530638], [-84.356924, 33.754707], [-84.3546476, 33.7558696], [-84.3497978, 33.7594961], [-84.3465028, 33.7